        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{log}_{8}\u2061(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u={log}_{8}\u2061(2x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}\u20618}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20618}\\,\u27f9\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u20618\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1/3$ | $2/3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{log}_{8}\u2061(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{1}^{2}{log}_{8}\u2061(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1/3}^{2/3}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u20618\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}\u20618{\\int }_{1/3}^{2/3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}\u20618\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1/3}^{2/3} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u20618\\cdot (\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20618\\end{aligned}\n$$",
